From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 10:48:34 -0400
Subject: feat: open Chrome as a stored account so Claude in Chrome follows it

`cswap switch` moves Claude Code to another account, but the Claude in
Chrome extension keeps acting as whoever it was signed in as (upstream
#256). The two never share a credential. Claude Code holds an OAuth pair
in the credential store; the extension runs its own OAuth flow with its
own client id and keeps its tokens in its own extension storage, using
the claude.ai web session only to re-authenticate silently after a
browser restart. Nothing outside the extension can switch its account,
and no cookie swap would move it without a re-login.

What Chrome does honor is `--user-data-dir`: a separate profile
directory is a separate, independently signed-in browser with its own
extensions. `cswap chrome <num|email ...>` gives every stored account a
permanent profile under `<backup>/chrome/<num>-<email slug>/` and opens
the browser against it, the same shape as `cswap desktop` for Claude
Desktop. Nothing reads, copies or migrates a login: Chrome owns
everything in the profile.

A profile of its own would otherwise mean signing in to every site
again and reinstalling every extension, so the first open of an account
copies the stock Chrome's last-used profile (`profile.last_used` in
`Local State`) into the new one and deletes its claude.ai cookies:
other sites stay signed in, extensions and their settings, bookmarks,
logins and history carry over, and only Claude is signed out. Caches,
locks and the open-tabs snapshot are left out. Databases are copied as
plain files with their journal or WAL, the way every tool that reads a
running Chrome's data does: Chrome holds most of them in exclusive
locking mode, and SQLite's backup API through Python retries a locked
source forever. Windows also gets `Local State`, which holds the key
the cookie jar is encrypted with there. On macOS the cookie jar is
behind Full Disk Access for terminals, so a refused read fails that
account with the two ways out (grant the access, or set
`chrome.cloneStockProfile` false to start empty) instead of leaving a
half copy. The Web Store page for the extension is only opened when the
profile does not already carry it. The user then signs in to that
account on claude.ai and clicks Sign in once in the Claude side panel;
the extension's silent re-auth is keyed to the previous account and
stops with a sign-in prompt, and its interactive sign-in takes the
account the browser is signed in as.

Each profile is named after its account (alias or email) and given a
window colour of its own by slot, through the untracked `Preferences`
keys, so two accounts' browsers are told apart at a glance; the last
exit is marked clean so a copied profile does not open with a restore
bar. `chrome.followSwitch` (off by default) makes an interactive
`cswap switch` bring the new account's browser forward when it already
has a profile; it never creates one and never fails the switch.

Chrome keeps one process singleton per profile directory, so a second
launch with the same `--user-data-dir` hands its URLs to the running
browser and exits; opening an account that is already open therefore
brings its browser forward through the same launch, on every platform.
The profile is found again by the exact `--user-data-dir=<dir>` token
on the command line, with the process helpers shared with desktop.py;
`--quit` sends TERM, then KILL after a timeout; a bare `cswap chrome`
lists which accounts have a browser open. `--no-first-run
--no-default-browser-check` keep Chrome's welcome flow and
default-browser prompt out of every new profile.

The browser is looked up in the stock Google Chrome install location
(`/Applications` or `~/Applications` on macOS, the Program Files and
LOCALAPPDATA roots on Windows, `google-chrome`, `google-chrome-stable`,
`chromium` or `chromium-browser` on PATH elsewhere) or the new
`chrome.appPath` setting. The extension runs in any Chromium browser,
so the setting may point at Edge, Brave or Chromium; on macOS a `.app`
bundle goes through `open -n`, anything else is started directly. The
copy always comes from the stock Google Chrome profile.

Removing an account refuses while its browser is open and otherwise
deletes the profile with the slot, and `purge` refuses while any browser
is open and lists the profiles it removes, the same way both treat
session and desktop profiles.

